 &nbsp; 
 
 Rivals are seeking to derail the $85 billion merger between Union Pacific and Norfolk Southern, which would create the first U.S. carrier running coast-to-coast, Axios Pro's Colin Campbell reports .

 Driving the news: BNSF last week said it will ask the Surface Transportation Board for access to 824 miles of trackage from Chicago to Harrisburg and Bethlehem, Pennsylvania.

 CPKC and CSX, meanwhile, want access to trackager between Kansas City to St. Louis — arguing that Union Pacific's recent deal with Canadian National doesn't go far enough to restore competition. The big picture: Union Pacific said in May that it would walk away from the merger if the STB grants such remedies, which would require it to pay a $2.5 billion breakup fee to Norfolk Southern.

 And that's no small thing, given that Union Pacific reported having only $2.11 billion of cash and cash equivalents as of June 30. Catch up quick: The deal was announced in July 2025 and is scheduled to close by early 2028.

 Zoom in: None of the access asks are a surprise, nor are they guaranteed regulatory approval. It's also unclear if they're mostly about adding trackage or just backdoor efforts to scuttle the merger.

 But they could put the STB in a bit of a political bind, given that Union Pacific has donated to the White House ballroom project. The STB could approve both the access requests and the merger, hoping that Trump isn't digging too deep into the weeds (i.e., merger approval = good). But if Union Pacific complains to the administration, let alone follows through on its threat to bail, then there could be blowback. Look ahead: The formal access asks are due by mid-November, with the STB expected to rule sometime next year.

 
 
